practical applications in machine learning, cryptography, and color models.","total_hours":8,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Vectors and Linear Combinations","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Introduction to vectors as ordered tuples of numbers; understanding linear combinations of vectors as scalar multiples summed together; geometric interpretation of vectors in 2D and 3D space.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linearity vs Non-linearity","Real-life Examples of Linearity"]},{"subtopic_name":"Visualization of Equations","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Geometric representation of linear equations as lines and planes; visualizing solution sets as intersection points, lines, or planes; understanding consistency and inconsistency visually.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vectors and Linear Combinations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Matrix Elimination","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Gaussian elimination and Gauss-Jordan elimination as systematic methods for solving linear systems; row operations and elementary matrices; forward elimination and back substitution.","prerequisites_within_course":["Visualization of Equations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Rank of a Matrix","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Definition of matrix rank as the maximum number of linearly independent rows or columns; rank from echelon forms; relationship between rank and solution existence for linear systems.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix Elimination"]},{"subtopic_name":"Echelon and Normal Forms","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Row echelon form, reduced row echelon form (RREF), and normal forms; steps to achieve echelon forms; identifying pivot positions and free variables.","prerequisites_within_course":["Rank of a Matrix"]},{"subtopic_name":"Homogeneous and Non-homogeneous Systems","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Understanding homogeneous systems (Ax=0) versus non-homogeneous systems (Ax=b); trivial and non-trivial solutions; connection between rank, nullity, and solution space dimension.","prerequisites_within_course":["Echelon and Normal Forms"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications: Machine Learning, Cryptography, and Color Models","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Practical applications including linear regression models in machine learning, simple encryption/decryption using linear transformations, and RGB color representation as vector spaces.","prerequisites_within_course":["Homogeneous and Non-homogeneous Systems"]}]},{"topic_name":"Vector Spaces and Linear Transformations","unit_number":3,"learning_intent":"Students will be able to understand abstract vector spaces and their properties, perform linear transformations between spaces, and apply the rank-nullity theorem to analyze transformation characteristics.","total_hours":12,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Vector Spaces and Subspaces","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Axiomatic definition of vector spaces over fields; common examples including R^n, function spaces, and polynomial spaces; definition and characterization of subspaces; testing for subspace validity.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vectors and Linear Combinations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Linear Span and Span Sets","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Understanding the span of a set of vectors as the set of all linear combinations; spanning sets; determining whether a vector lies in the span of a given set; span as a subspace.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vector Spaces and Subspaces"]},{"subtopic_name":"Linear Independence and Dependence","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2,"description":"Definition of linear independence and dependence; testing for independence using linear combinations equal to zero; spanning sets and minimal spanning sets; relationship with dimension.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear Span and Span Sets"]},{"subtopic_name":"Basis and Dimension","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2,"description":"Basis as a linearly independent spanning set; standard bases for R^n; coordinates of vectors relative to a basis; dimension as the number of vectors in any basis; dimension theorem for subspaces.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear Independence and Dependence"]},{"subtopic_name":"Linear Mappings","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Definition of linear transformations; matrix representations of linear maps; properties preserved by linear transformations; examples including rotations, projections, and reflections.","prerequisites_within_course":["Basis and Dimension"]},{"subtopic_name":"Kernel, Image, and Null Space","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Kernel (null space) as the set of vectors mapping to zero; image (range) as the set of all outputs; nullity and rank of a transformation; solving systems using kernel concepts.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear Mappings"]},{"subtopic_name":"Rank-Nullity Theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Statement and proof of the rank-nullity theorem (dimension of kernel plus dimension of image equals dimension of domain); applications to solving linear systems and understanding transformation properties.","prerequisites_within_course":["Kernel, Image, and Null Space"]},{"subtopic_name":"Matrix Similarity and Change of Basis","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Similar matrices representing the same linear transformation under different bases; similarity transformation formulas; diagonalization as a change of basis to simplify matrix representation.","prerequisites_within_course":["Rank-Nullity Theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Inner Product Spaces","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2,"description":"Definition and properties of inner products; Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; angle between vectors; orthogonality and orthonormal bases; extensions beyond Euclidean spaces.","prerequisites_within_course":["Basis and Dimension"]},{"subtopic_name":"Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization Process","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Orthogonalizing a set of vectors to produce an orthonormal basis; step-by-step orthogonal projection procedures; applications to least squares approximation and QR decomposition.","prerequisites_within_course":["Inner Product Spaces"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications: Data Smoothing and Image Scaling","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Using linear transformations for smoothing noisy data through projection; applying matrix operations and interpolation for image scaling and transformation in computer graphics.","prerequisites_within_course":["Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization Process"]}]},{"topic_name":"Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors","unit_number":4,"learning_intent":"Students will be able to compute and interpret eigenvalues and eigenvectors, apply the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, diagonalize matrices, and use quadratic forms with the least squares method for practical applications in facial recognition and search engines.","total_hours":8,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Characteristic Polynomial and Cayley-Hamilton Theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Computing the characteristic polynomial from det(A-lambda I); finding eigenvalues as roots; statement and verification of the Cayley-Hamilton theorem that every matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation.","prerequisites_within_course":["Rank-Nullity Theorem","Matrix Similarity and Change of Basis"]},{"subtopic_name":"Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Definition and computation of eigenvectors and eigenvalues; eigenspaces as solution sets; algebraic versus geometric multiplicity; finding eigenvectors for given eigenvalues.","prerequisites_within_course":["Characteristic Polynomial and Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Visual understanding of eigenvectors as directions unchanged by the transformation; eigenvalues as scaling factors; transformation effects including stretching, compressing, and reflection along eigenvector directions.","prerequisites_within_course":["Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"]},{"subtopic_name":"Matrix Diagonalization","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Diagonalization conditions: sufficient linearly independent eigenvectors; procedure for diagonalization A = PDP^(-1); powers and functions of diagonalizable matrices; limitations when diagonalization is not possible.","prerequisites_within_course":["Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"]},{"subtopic_name":"Symmetric Matrices and Quadratic Forms","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Properties of symmetric matrices: real eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenvectors; spectral theorem for symmetric matrices; quadratic forms as x^T Ax; classification as positive definite, negative definite, or indefinite; change of variables to eliminate cross terms.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix Diagonalization","Inner Product Spaces"]},{"subtopic_name":"Least Squares Method","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Solving overdetermined systems using the least squares approach; normal equations; orthogonal projection onto column space; applications to data fitting and linear regression.","prerequisites_within_course":["Symmetric Matrices and Quadratic Forms","Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization Process"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications: Facial Recognition, Feature Extraction, and Search Engines","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Using eigenvalues for dimensionality reduction in face recognition systems; feature extraction in image processing; eigenvector-based ranking methods in search engine algorithms.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix Diagonalization","Least Squares Method"]}]},{"topic_name":"Singular Value Decomposition","unit_number":5,"learning_intent":"Students will be able to perform and apply singular value decomposition, understand spectral decomposition, use rank-k approximations, and apply the power method for applications including PCA and data clustering.","total_hours":8,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Spectral Decomposition","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1,"description":"Spectral decomposition for symmetric matrices using orthogonal eigenvectors; expressing a symmetric matrix as a sum of rank-one projection matrices scaled by eigenvalues; understanding the decomposition in terms of matrix actions.","prerequisites_within_course":["Symmetric Matrices and Quadratic Forms"]},{"subtopic_name":"Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.5,"description":"Full SVD: A = U Sigma V^T with orthogonal matrices U and V and diagonal matrix Sigma; singular values as square roots of eigenvalues of A^T A; relationship between singular vectors and eigenvectors; compact SVD for rank-r matrices.","prerequisites_within_course":["Spectral Decomposition"]},{"subtopic_name":"Rank-k Approximation and the Power Method","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Truncated SVD for rank-k approximation using the k largest singular values; Eckart-Young theorem on optimal low-rank approximation; power iteration for finding dominant singular vectors; convergence behavior of the power method.","prerequisites_within_course":["Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)"]},{"subtopic_name":"Principal Component Analysis (PCA)","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2,"description":"PCA as an application of SVD fo